course: everyday
lesson: 235
title: Rain, floods and storms
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Rain is fun to play in, but water hides things. A puddle you cannot see the bottom of may hide a hole, an open drain or broken glass, and fast water in a gutter or ditch is much stronger than it looks.
explain: Floodwater is the big danger. Just 15 cm of moving water can knock a person off their feet, and 30 cm can float a car. That is why safety services say «Turn around, don't drown»: never walk, ride or drive through it.
explain: In a thunderstorm, lightning strikes tall things and travels through water, metal and pipes. Indoors, keep out of the bath and shower and off corded phones; outdoors with no building near, get into a hard-topped car or crouch low away from trees, water and metal fences.
explain: Big storms such as hurricanes and cyclones are forecast days ahead, so families have time to prepare and to leave if told to. A warning is a gift of time: listen and act.
explain: Example: a boy in Houston, Texas, sees a road covered in brown water on the way home. The water looks only ankle deep, but his dad turns the car around, because nobody can see how deep or fast it really is.
fact: Jump only in shallow puddles away from roads, and never in water you cannot see the bottom of.
fact: Deep or flooded puddles can hide holes, drains and sharp things.
fact: On a wet day walk away from the kerb, because cars splash and wet roads make it harder for cars to stop.
fact: Keep away from storm drains, ditches and culverts, because fast water can sweep a child into a drain pipe.
fact: Just 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down and 30 cm can float a car, so turn around and never walk, ride or drive through floodwater.
fact: Never play or swim in floodwater, because it contains sewage, chemicals and hidden dangers.
fact: To see a rainbow, stand with the sun behind you and look towards the rain; it forms when sunlight bends in raindrops, and you can never reach its end, because it moves as you move.
fact: In a thunderstorm with no building nearby, get into a hard-topped car, or crouch low away from tall trees, water and metal fences.
fact: Lightning hits tall objects and travels through water, metal, pipes and phone wires.
fact: During a thunderstorm avoid baths, showers, washing up and corded phones until it passes.
fact: When big hailstones fall, go indoors and stay away from windows and skylights, because hail can hurt people and break glass.
fact: Before a hurricane or cyclone, bring in loose garden things, pack an emergency kit and follow evacuation orders, because strong winds turn loose objects into missiles and storm surges flood coasts.
fact: When a severe weather warning comes on the radio, TV or phone, listen and do what it says with your family; warnings give time to get safe.
exercise: Which puddles are safe to jump in? | Shallow ones away from roads where you can see the bottom | Deep brown ones, because they always make the biggest and best splash | Any puddle at all, because rainwater is always clean | Puddles near the road, where the water collects
exercise: Why should you not jump into a puddle when you cannot see its bottom? | It may hide a hole, a drain or something sharp | The water is always much colder in the deep puddles | Deep puddles make your shoes far too heavy to walk in | Muddy water always gives you a cold the very next day
exercise: Walking home on a wet day, where should you walk? | Away from the kerb, as far from the road as you can | Right at the very edge of the kerb, so you can see the cars coming | In the gutter, where the rainwater runs away | On the road itself, where it is smoother
exercise: Water is rushing along the gutter towards a storm drain. What do you do? | Keep away, because fast water can sweep a child into the drain | Float a stick in it and follow it all the way to the drain | Step in to check how strong the water is | Stand on top of the drain cover to watch the water go down
exercise: How much moving water can knock a person off their feet? | About 15 cm, around the height of your ankle | About one metre, as high as your chest | About 2 metres, more than a grown-up is tall | Moving water can never knock an adult down
exercise: A path is covered by floodwater. What should you do? | Turn around and find another way | Walk through slowly, holding your bike | Ride fast so you get across quickly | Wade in slowly to test how deep it is
exercise: Why should children never play in floodwater? | It contains sewage, chemicals and hidden dangers | It is too warm and makes you feel tired and sleepy | It is fine once the rain has stopped falling | It only matters if you cannot swim
exercise: Where should you look to see a rainbow? | Towards the rain, with the sun behind you | Straight at the sun, through the rain | Down at the ground, under the clouds | Anywhere, because rainbows appear all around you
exercise: Why can you never reach the end of a rainbow? | It is sunlight bent in raindrops, and it moves as you move | It is too far away, always in another country | It sinks into the ground as soon as you get close to it | It ends in the sea, where nobody can walk
exercise: A thunderstorm starts and there is no building nearby. Where is safest? | Inside a hard-topped car, or crouched low in open ground | Under the tallest tree you can find, where you keep dry | Holding on tight to a metal fence | Swimming in the lake until the storm has completely ended
exercise: Why should you not take a bath during a thunderstorm? | Lightning can travel through the water pipes | Thunder makes the bath water go cold very quickly | The bath might overflow from all the rain coming in | Bubbles in the bath attract lightning into the house
exercise: Big hailstones start to fall. What should you do? | Go indoors and keep away from windows and skylights | Stand under a skylight to watch them bounce | Stay outside and catch them in a bucket to see how big they are | Shelter under the glass roof of the nearest bus stop
exercise: A hurricane is forecast for your town in Florida. How can you help? | Bring in loose garden things and help pack an emergency kit | Leave the trampoline out in the garden, because it is heavy | Wait until the wind starts before getting anything ready at all | Go to the beach to watch the big waves
exercise: Why must loose garden things be brought in before a cyclone? | Strong winds turn them into flying missiles | Rain makes them rust far too quickly | The neighbours might come and take them away in the storm | They make the storm surge higher
exercise: A severe weather warning flashes on your mum's phone. What should you do? | Listen to the warning and do what it says together | Ignore it, because those warnings are usually wrong | Go outside first to see if the weather is really bad yet | Turn the phone off quickly so that it stops beeping at everyone
test: A girl in Leeds sees a puddle across the whole footpath, brown and muddy. What is wisest? | Go around it, because she cannot see what is under the water | Jump straight in, since footpaths are always flat underneath | Wade in slowly to test the depth with her feet first | Run through it fast so she gets less wet
test: Which is true about floodwater? | Even shallow moving water can knock you down or float a car | Cars are heavy enough to drive safely through any flood they meet | Floodwater is clean, because it is rain | Only deep water over your head is dangerous
test: Thunder rumbles while a boy in Colorado is washing up. What should he do? | Stop and wait until the storm passes | Finish quickly before the lightning gets close | Wear rubber gloves to stay safe | Fill the sink higher with water
test: Why does crouching low in the open help in a lightning storm? | Lightning tends to hit the tallest things around | Lying flat on the wet grass is always the safest of all | Lightning cannot reach the ground at all | Being low down keeps you dry from the rain
test: A friend says, «Let's float our boats down the ditch, the water is really fast today!» What do you say? | No, fast water in a ditch can sweep us into a pipe | Yes, but only if we all stand right at the very edge | Yes, fast water is safer than slow water because it is clean | Yes, as long as we hold hands tightly when we are in the water
test: Your family is told to evacuate before a cyclone in Queensland. What is right? | Follow the order and leave with your family | Stay home, because houses are always safe in storms | Wait at home to see how strong the wind gets first | Go down to the coast to watch the surge come in
test: Why should you stay away from windows when hail falls? | Hail can break glass and the pieces can cut you | Hailstones melt faster near the warm windows | Windows make the noise much louder | It is only for houses that have skylights in the roof
test: On a rainy walk to school, why is it safer to walk away from the kerb? | Cars splash and need longer to stop on wet roads | The pavement is always drier near the road | Drivers see you much better when it is raining | Puddles are always deeper away from the road, so it is drier there
test: A boy in Ireland wants to run to the end of a rainbow. What is true? | He can never reach it, because it moves as he moves | He will find it waiting at the bottom of the hill | It is only a few streets away from him | He can reach it if the rain keeps falling long enough
test: road's flooded but only a bit, can we ride thru on the bikes | No, turn around, moving water can knock you down | Yes, if it's under your knees it's fine | Yes, ride fast so you don't fall | Only if a car went through first n made it ok
test: thunder n lightning, can i have my bath now | No, wait until the storm has passed | Yes, baths are totally safe in storms | Yes, if you keep the bathroom light off | Only a quick shower, that's different
test: storm coming, we're in the park. hide under that big tree? | No, get to a building or hard-topped car | Yes, trees keep you dry and safe | Yes, hug the trunk tight till it stops | Stand by the metal fence instead
test: wanna catch the end of the rainbow | You can never reach it, it moves with you | Run fast enough and you'll get there | It ends on top of the nearest hill | Only adults can reach it
explain: A family in Texas drove into a flooded road thinking it was shallow; the water swept the car off the road and they had to be rescued from the roof.
explain: A boy in Australia sheltered under a tall tree in a thunderstorm; lightning struck the tree and he was thrown to the ground with burns.
fact: Most people who die in floods are in cars or are walking through water; just a little fast water can sweep a car away.
fact: Lightning often strikes tall things, so standing under a tree in a storm is dangerous.
test: road's flooded, dad says it's only a bit deep | Turn around, floodwater can sweep the car away | Drive through fast so the water can't stop u | Wade across first | Wait in the middle till the water goes down
test: thunder, can we shelter under the big tree | No, go inside a building or car | Yes, it keeps you dry n safe | Yes, if you lie down under it | Only the biggest tree
test: Why is it dangerous to play in floodwater? | It can be fast, deep and full of dirt and germs | It is too cold for children to play in for long | Floods are boring | It is only dangerous at night, when it is hard to see
test: did u hear a guy drove his ute (a small pickup truck) through the flooded road and it just floated off, they had to get him out by helicopter? real lesson? | Turn around; never drive or walk through floodwater | Drive fast so the car doesn't have time to float | Only small cars float, not big ones like utes | Floodwater is fine if it's shallow-looking
test: apparently a kid got sucked into a storm drain playing in the rain and came out in the river miles away. what's the true lesson? | Keep away from storm drains and ditches when it rains | Storm drains are fun slides when it rains | Only babies are small enough to fit in storm drains | Drains are always safe once the rain has stopped falling
test: my mate says if u touch a tree in a storm the lightning goes into u and turns u to ash. what's exaggerated and what's true? | Ash is made up, but trees in storms are dangerous | All true, lightning only hits people | All made up, trees are the safest place | Lightning never hits trees
exam: Identify the depth of moving water that may suffice to cause a car to float. | About thirty centimetres | About three metres | About one centimetre | About ten metres
exam: Which of the following constitutes appropriate conduct during a thunderstorm indoors? | Baths, showers, washing up and corded phones are avoided | One bathes, since water is entirely safe indoors | One stands by an open window | One uses a corded telephone freely throughout the storm
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning floodwater. | It may contain sewage, chemicals and hidden dangers | It is clean, being rainwater | It is safe for swimming provided that it is warm | It is safe once it has stopped rising, even when deep
exam: Ascertain the position from which a rainbow may be observed. | With the sun behind one, facing the rain | Facing directly into the sun | Standing directly beneath the rain cloud | Only from the summit of a high hill
